a new book explores the challenges of  modern boyhood and it argues  our approach to raising boys has failed  and the time now is to change it to  raise a boy classrooms  locker rooms bedrooms and the hidden  struggles of american boyhood  is published by atria it's an imprint of  simon schuster which is a division of  vicom cbs it's an important book though  author and washington post reporter emma  brown says  that in the wake of the metoo movement  she wanted to know  how will i raise my son to be different  and she joins us now for an interview  you were seeing first on cbs this  morning emma good morning to you  uh so we talk a lot on this show about  what men do  to each other to women but we don't  spend much time thinking about  why men are the way they are and this  book does that it comes along at exactly  the right time and i was hoping you  could begin  by just telling viewers what it was you  thought was missing from your  parental toolkit when you looked down at  your son gus and thought  how am i going to do this well thank you  i  you know when my daughter was born three  years before my son  i had such strong gut instincts about  how i wanted to parent her  to resist the messages that get beamed  at girls from the time that they're born  and i realized i had none of those gut  instincts for my son because  i have never been a boy and you know i  had never  really even grappled with the idea that  boys face pressures or stresses in the  way that girls do  and so this book was a chance for me to  explore that yeah it's interesting that  you know you think that certain things  are innately different between boys and  girls but  to a large extent they come out the same  and then it's things that we teach them  that make them different and actually  i'll give you a really good example from  my household this week  my son teddy's favorite book currently  is a book called  boys dance it's by the american ballet  theater  it's about boys doing ballet but as your  book points out as we all know  at a certain point he will start school  and he'll start getting this message  that certain things maybe even ballet  is girly and not for him what are  parents supposed to do to stop that  process  yeah i mean i think you know shame is a  really corrosive force  and boys get shamed for doing anything  that is seen to be girly and that is one  of the  ingredients that i think is um is  you know adds up to pretty poor outcomes  for men in america  both in terms of physical and mental  health boy so many boys told me  um that they were told by their parents  never to cry because they're boys and so  when we tell boys to  shut off the parts of themselves that  are human uh  that is you know that's that's how we  end up with  um men who die by suicide at four times  the rate of women  shorter lives for men um boys are really  struggle  and we need to help them and as you say  parents need help from the institutions  that help raise our sons  including schools sports teams faith  communities  absolutely yeah help boys become a  complete person as you argue  consent is obviously a big issue parents  know  that you've got to teach the difference  between yes and no  but it's more complicated you have a  phrase in the book uh that  no is not always spelled n-o  explain that absolutely well you know  what  a boy a young man i met um  a college man told me the way we talk  about consent gets translated to boys  like this he says you teach us no means  no and that teach just  teaches us we need to get her to say yes  get the  girl we're with to say yes and um and  that is exactly what you're talking  about you know girls um  girls and boys express themselves  verbally and non-verbally  and so if we're not careful the way that  we talk about consent can just  teach boys um to to ignore some of the  non-verbal  signs that they get uh that are very  that can be very clear  um and instead just sort of look for a  way to  coerce or pressure so we've got to find  a new way to talk about  what consent is and it's about listening  right it's about listening to the person  you're with and respecting  what you hear from them whether you hear  that verbally or non-verbally  emma part of the reason i really endorse  this book for parents even though it's  not a parenting book is because it  helped you come up with a mantra an idea  for how you wanted to raise your son  so for your daughter you were telling  her strong and fearless  for your son you came up with strong and  gentle  i love that yeah you know  i i feel like it gives him access to the  best parts of what we  traditionally think of as feminine and  the best parts of what we have  traditionally thought of as masculine  and he should have access to the best of  both of those worlds  all right if you're a parent and you're  anxious about raising a boy in this  world this book will take your  temperature down i endorse it emma brown  thank you very much for being here we  appreciate it to raise a boy  is on sale right now
 

